<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wrocław</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Breslau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wrocław (German Breslau) is the largest city of Silesia, on the Oder river in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. A bishopric from 1000 and the capital of the Piast dukes of Silesia, it later belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took it in 1742. After 1945 it became part of Poland, its German population was expelled and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, resettled it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wroclaw.jpg" alt="Wrocław" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jar.ciurus · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wroclaw-_Most_Grunwaldzki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…I, published by the British East India Company in 1814–1818; Bulaq, printed by the Egyptian government in 1835; <b>Breslau</b>, prepared by Christian Maximilian Habicht with Mordecai ibn al-Najjar from 1825, whose “Tunisian manuscript” was later revealed as a forgery by al-Najjar; and Calcutta II, which claimed an older…</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The next year Hoffmann lost his post as librarian and professor in <b>Breslau</b> because of this and other works, and lived in hiding until he was pardoned after the revolutions of 1848.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote>West of <b>Breslau</b> in Silesia, Frederick the Great used the oblique order of attack to rout an Austrian army about twice the size of his own under Prince Charles of Lorraine.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-5">5 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors also gained Brzeg and at times <b>Wrocław</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-legnica">Duchy of Legnica</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops of <b>Wrocław</b> held the castellany of Nysa and Otmuchów from the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-nysa">Duchy of Nysa</a></li><li><blockquote>In the division of the Duchy of Głogów among the sons of Henry III, Konrad I received Oleśnica (Oels), east of <b>Wrocław</b>, and ruled it as a separate duchy by 1320.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olesnica">Duchy of Oleśnica</a></li></ul></section>
